          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-e5c29cd067d31218\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite’s “rappelez-vous seulement que je vous aime” (“just remember that I love you”) explicitly presents her love for Armand as an enduring emotional state he should treat as true. It supports her attempt to reassure him and secure his agreement, but the core utterance does not itself substantially undertake a commitment. Its deliberate, reason-giving formulation supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal has a persuasive function within Marguerite’s argument, but persuasion and reassurance do not by themselves make the love utterance performative.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite directly tells Armand, in spoken dialogue, to remember that she loves him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite is trying to overcome Armand’s jealousy and suspicion about her undisclosed financial scheme. She invokes her love as a reason for him to trust her, stop worrying, and consent to their proposed summer together. This strategic and reassuring use does not displace the utterance’s dominant force as an avowal of an emotional state; nor does the surrounding intensity (“j’ai la folie de vous aimer”) make the highlighted sentence an expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“rappelez-vous seulement que je vous aime, et ne vous inquiétez de rien”\",\"supports\":\"The imperative “remember” frames the love proposition as something Armand should accept as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite introduces the appeal with “Voyons, raisonnons un peu” and explains how she will reconcile her affairs with her love for Armand.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance occurs in a controlled argument intended to reassure and persuade, rather than as a reflex-like verbal discharge; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite asks whether the plan is agreed immediately after telling Armand to remember that she loves him.\",\"supports\":\"Her avowal is strategically used to obtain trust and agreement, but the commitment concerns the surrounding plan rather than being enacted by the core love utterance itself; this supports P=0.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter XIII of La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified core is the direct present-tense “je vous aime” near the end of Marguerite’s speech.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The surrounding persuasion, reassurance, jealousy, and proposed shared future are contextual functions adequately described without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
